{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the rain begins, when the air turns metallic and the streetlights seem to hesitate. It is not quite day and not quite night, and for a few minutes the city feels suspended between decisions. People walk faster without knowing why, pigeons scatter from a roof in a single grey breath, and somewhere a window bangs open and shut. I like to stand on my small balcony then, holding a mug I have forgotten to drink from, watching that charged pause. It reminds me that most of life is not made of beginnings or endings, but of these in-between weather systems moving through a person.\n\nWe wait for jobs, for apologies, for test results, for someone to text back, for grief to loosen its grip. We tell ourselves the waiting is empty, a corridor between rooms, but I have learned more in corridors than I ever learned at destinations. In waiting, I notice who I become when nothing is demanded of me except patience. Sometimes I become generous, humming while I wash dishes; sometimes I become small and sharp, refreshing a screen as if urgency could summon mercy. Then the rain finally arrives, not dramatically, but in scattered taps on the railing, and the tension dissolves into an ordinary sound. Nothing has been solved, yet something has passed, and I can breathe again in the cleaner air. That, perhaps, is enough: to learn how to stay, unhurried, while the sky changes its mind.",
